  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable this weekend.\n\nHigh pressure will build near and south of the region through\nthe weekend as an upper trough axis pulls away to the east.\nBrisk west to northwest winds will usher in cooler, more\nseasonable temperatures. Winds will gradually diminish this\nevening, except at higher elevations and over the waters. \nSunday will still be breezy, but about 5-10 mph lower.\n\nWave clouds/stratocu likely persist west of I-81 into the\nevening. It will be mainly clear and dry otherwise.\n\nThe central Virginia piedmont will be closer to the high and\nhave a chance to decouple (winds going light/calm) tonight and\nagain Sunday night. Near freezing temperatures are expected as a  \nresult. Frost/freeze headlines have been issued, and if current\nguidance is correct, the growing season may end tonight for\nseveral zones in central Virginia.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry and mild Monday through Wednesday.\n\nOne dry front will cross by Monday, leading to breezy northwest\nwinds returning. Cold advection behind this front is modest, so\nhigh temperatures will likely be similar to Sunday when combined\nwith downsloping effects.\n\nAnother dry/weak front will move across the region Tuesday. \nTemperatures will be about 5 to 8 degrees above average as there\ncould be a warming surface wind out of the southwest. Highs \nwill reach the middle 60s to lower 70s with upper 50s to near 60\nin the mountains. Lows Tuesday night will drop into the upper \n30s to mid 40s. Once the front moves through, a surface high \npressure will quickly build into and across the region for \nWednesday. This will allow for temperatures to remain above \naverage by a few degrees and the day to be dry. Highs in the \nmiddle to upper 60s with middle 50s in the mountains. The high \nquickly moves east by Wednesday night. Lows mid 30s to mid 40s. \nThe colder temperatures could support some frost/freeze concerns\nwhere the growing season continues mainly east of the Blue Ridge.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Colder and unsettled Thursday and Friday.\n\nLooking into the Thursday and Friday timeframe, an area of low \npressure could move along the Ohio Valley or Lower Great Lakes and \npush into parts of the Mid-Atlantic region. An associated cold \nfront will trail the main low. Some rain showers could accompany\nthe low and front. During the same timeframe, a secondary low \npressure system (or multiple smaller low pressure systems) will\ntry to catch up to the first low and thus intensify it near the\nEastern Seaboard. There are some discrepancies with the many \nmodels in the position, timing, and intensity of the secondary \nlow pressure system. As for now, there is a higher confidence \nthat the weather could become unsettled with rain showers or \nperiods of rain, as well as some higher elevation snow or \nrain/snow mix. It is too early to tell where or to what extent \nany snow will evolve in any areas further to the east and \nsoutheast in our region. Highs Thursday only in the upper 50s to\nlower 60s and Friday will have highs in the upper 40s to middle\n50s with some mid/upper 30s for highs in the Appalachians.",
